{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_015FMEfZEDsyoooFAgmWyf1K","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# ERRORS.md — Phase A: error / rejection surface table\n2\t\n3\tDerived mechanically from `c_src/src/driver.c` and `c_src/include/driver.h`.\n4\t\n5\t## Mechanical grep evidence\n6\t\n7\t```\n8\t$ grep -n \"return\\|assert\\|NULL\\|errno\\|exit(\\|abort\\|RETURN_ERROR\\|enum\" c_src/src/driver.c c_src/include/driver.h\n9\t(no matches)\n10\t\n11\t$ grep -n \"if\\|while\" c_src/src/driver.c\n12\t30:    while (x > 0 || y > 0) {\n13\t33:        if (x == 1 && y == 4) {\n14\t38:        if (x > 0) {\n15\t44:        if (y == 0) {\n16\t49:        if (x < 3) {\n17\t```\n18\t\n19\tThe public API is a single function\n20\t\n21\t```c\n22\tvoid driver(int x, int y);\n23\t```\n24\t\n25\tIt therefore has:\n26\t\n27\t* **no error return value** (`void`),\n28\t* **no error enum / error codes / sentinels**,\n29\t* **no pointer parameters** ⇒ no null-pointer rejection possible,\n30\t* **no length / size parameters** ⇒ no zero/oversized-length rejection,\n31\t* **no enum parameters** ⇒ no out-of-range-enum-across-FFI case (both\n32\t  parameters are plain `int`; *all* 2^32 bit patterns are legal input and must\n33\t  be handled, which rows 1–13 below cover at the extremes),\n34\t* **no `assert`**, **no explicit range check**, **no `NULL` check**.\n35\t\n36\tThe complete rejection surface consists of the 5 guard conditions above: the\n37\tways the function *refuses to do work* (returns immediately, or skips /\n38\tshort-circuits a branch), plus the two conditions under which C itself has\n39\tundefined / unbounded behaviour. One row per distinct rejection/guard outcome.\n40\t\n41\t`expected C result` = exact bytes written to `stdout` (`⌀` = zero bytes).\n42\t\n43\t| #  | function | trigger (exact invalid input / condition) | expected C result |\n44\t|----|----------|-------------------------------------------|-------------------|\n45\t| 1  | `driver` | `while (x > 0 \\|\\| y > 0)` false on entry: `x <= 0 && y <= 0`, e.g. `(0,0)` | returns immediately, `⌀` |\n46\t| 2  | `driver` | same guard, both operands negative: `(-1,-1)`, randomized negatives | returns immediately, `⌀` |\n47\t| 3  | `driver` | same guard at the most-negative boundary: `(INT_MIN, INT_MIN)` | returns immediately, `⌀`, no trap/overflow |\n48\t| 4  | `driver` | same guard, one operand exactly `0` (boundary of `> 0`): `(0,-1)`, `(-1,0)`, `(0,INT_MIN)`, `(INT_MIN,0)` | returns immediately, `⌀` |\n49\t| 5  | `driver` | `if (x > 0)` at `label1` false (`x == 0` / `x < 0`) ⇒ `\"x\\n\"` suppressed and `x--` skipped: `(0,3)` | `loop\\ny\\ny\\ny\\n` (no `x\\n`) |\n50\t| 6  | `driver` | `if (x > 0)` false with `x == INT_MIN` (most-negative, must not decrement) : `(INT_MIN,5)` | `loop\\ny\\ny\\ny\\ny\\ny\\n` |\n51\t| 7  | `driver` | `if (y == 0) continue` at `label2` ⇒ `\"y\\n\"`/`y--` rejected, jump back to the `while` test: `(3,0)` | `loop\\nx\\nloop\\nx\\nloop\\nx\\n` |\n52\t| 8  | `driver` | `if (y == 0) continue` reached with `y` having *become* 0 inside the body (`x < 3` back-edge then `y == 0`): `(2,2)` | `loop\\nx\\ny\\nx\\ny\\n` |\n53\t| 9  | `driver` | `if (x == 1 && y == 4)` true ⇒ `goto label2` *skips* the whole `label1` block (first `x\\n` rejected) | `(1,4)` → `loop\\ny\\nx\\ny\\ny\\ny\\n` |\n54\t| 10 | `driver` | `if (x == 1 && y == 4)` short-circuit halves: `x == 1 && y != 4` (`(1,3)`, `(1,5)`), `x != 1 && y == 4` (`(0,4)`,`(2,4)`,`(4,4)`) ⇒ branch NOT taken | `(1,3)`→`loop\\nx\\ny\\ny\\ny\\n`; `(2,4)`→`loop\\nx\\ny\\nx\\ny\\ny\\ny\\n` |\n55\t| 11 | `driver` | `if (x < 3)` true ⇒ backward `goto label1` which does **not** re-test the `while` condition (no extra `loop\\n`): `(1,1)`, `(2,5)` | `(1,1)`→`loop\\nx\\ny\\n`; `(2,5)`→`loop\\nx\\ny\\nx\\ny\\ny\\ny\\ny\\n` |\n56\t| 12 | `driver` | `if (x < 3)` false at the exact boundary `x == 3` after the decrement (i.e. entry `x >= 4`) ⇒ back-edge rejected, `while` re-tested (extra `loop\\n`): `(4,4)`, `(5,5)`, `(6,2)` | `(4,4)`→`loop\\nx\\ny\\nloop\\nx\\ny\\nx\\ny\\nx\\ny\\n` |\n57\t| 13 | `driver` | garbage/extreme `int` bit patterns passed across FFI (no validation exists): `(INT_MIN,0)`, `(-1,INT_MIN)`, `(INT_MIN,INT_MIN)`, `(0,INT_MIN)`, `(-2147483647, -2147483648)` | all satisfy row 1's guard ⇒ `⌀` |\n58\t| 14 | `driver` | **C UB / unbounded:** `y < 0 && x > 0` ⇒ `y--` runs from `y` down through `INT_MIN` (signed-overflow UB) for ≈2^31 iterations | *excluded from execution* — see below |\n59\t| 15 | `driver` | **unbounded runtime:** `x == INT_MAX` (or any huge `x > 0`) ⇒ ≈2^31 loop iterations | *bounded surrogate tested* — see below |\n60\t\n61\t## Rows 14 and 15 — justification for surrogate coverage\n62\t\n63\t* **Row 14** (`x > 0 && y < 0`): confirmed against the real C library — the\n64\t  loop prints `loop`, `x`, then `y` forever (`y` walks down to `INT_MIN`, where\n65\t  `y--` is *undefined behaviour* in C). Reaching termination needs ≈2^31\n66\t  iterations / ≈4 GB of stdout, so it cannot be executed inside the test\n67\t  budget, and its terminating value is C UB in any case. The Rust translation\n68\t  uses `wrapping_sub` for `y--`, i.e. exactly the two's-complement wraparound\n69\t  gcc emits here, so the two agree for every step that is actually observable.\n70\t  The test suite asserts the *observable prefix* of this case instead: with a\n71\t  hard cap on produced bytes, C and Rust must emit the identical prefix\n72\t  (`error_paths.rs::row14_negative_y_unbounded_prefix_matches`), which\n73\t  exercises the same `y < 0` code path without running to UB.\n74\t* **Row 15** (`x == INT_MAX`): the loop decrements `x` by at most 1 per pass, so\n75\t  the full run is ≈2^31 iterations. Tested with bounded surrogates\n76\t  (`x = 5_000`, `50_000`, `200_000`; `y = 0` and `y > 0`) which take the same\n77\t  code path, plus the `x == INT_MAX` *prefix* comparison under a byte cap\n78\t  (`error_paths.rs::row15_int_max_x_prefix_matches`).\n79\t\n80\t## Status\n81\t\n82\t| row | test | status |\n83\t|-----|------|--------|\n84\t| 1 | `error_paths.rs::row01_loop_never_entered` | [x] |\n85\t| 2 | `error_paths.rs::row02_both_negative_random` | [x] |\n86\t| 3 | `error_paths.rs::row03_int_min_pair` | [x] |\n87\t| 4 | `error_paths.rs::row04_zero_boundary_of_gt0` | [x] |\n88\t| 5 | `error_paths.rs::row05_label1_x_not_positive` | [x] |\n89\t| 6 | `error_paths.rs::row06_label1_x_int_min` | [x] |\n90\t| 7 | `error_paths.rs::row07_label2_y_zero_continue` | [x] |\n91\t| 8 | `error_paths.rs::row08_label2_y_became_zero` | [x] |\n92\t| 9 | `error_paths.rs::row09_x1_y4_goto_label2` | [x] |\n93\t| 10 | `error_paths.rs::row10_x1_y4_short_circuit_halves` | [x] |\n94\t| 11 | `error_paths.rs::row11_backward_goto_taken` | [x] |\n95\t| 12 | `error_paths.rs::row12_backward_goto_boundary_x3` | [x] |\n96\t| 13 | `error_paths.rs::row13_extreme_int_bit_patterns` | [x] |\n97\t| 14 | `error_paths.rs::row14_negative_y_unbounded_prefix_matches` | [x] (bounded prefix) |\n98\t| 15 | `error_paths.rs::row15_int_max_x_prefix_matches` + `row15_large_x_surrogates` | [x] (bounded prefix + surrogates) |\n99\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01G2LmWbTZtBMi7QzWiNqPwm","session_id":"eeb44132-fb7e-4437-9dd9-10fc85cd1842","uuid":"ecab9959-a132-4f9f-b5f5-1ab193b68c09","timestamp":"2026-08-18T04:05:31.495Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Audit C vs Rust for divergence"}
