{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"abd8dcf8bfe9f0567","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01G2LmWbTZtBMi7QzWiNqPwm","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-1bfdsY/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-1bfdsY-translated-rust/eeb44132-fb7e-4437-9dd9-10fc85cd1842/tasks/abd8dcf8bfe9f0567.output","summary":"## (a) VERDICT\n\n**No divergence in the translated logic.** The Rust `driver` is control-flow-isomorphic to the C, and I could not find any `(x, y)` whose stdout byte stream differs. **One real divergence does exist**, but it is in the *stdio call choice*, not the algorithm, and only in the `target/debug` artifact — details below.\n\n**Hand trace.** The C body has 4 CFG nodes; the Rust reproduces each edge exactly:\n\n| C | Rust |\n|---|---|\n| `COND: x>0\\|\\|y>0 → BODY : EXIT` | outer `while x > 0 \\|\\| y > 0` |\n| `BODY: print\"loop\"; (x==1&&y==4) → L2 : L1` | `Label::Body` arm (`label = Label2; continue` vs `label = Label1` fall-out) |\n| `L1: if x>0 {print\"x\"; x--} → L2` (fall-through) | `Label::Label1` arm, then `label = Label2` |\n| `L2: y==0 → COND` (`continue`) | `break` out of inner `loop` → re-tests `while` |\n| `L2: print\"y\"; y--; (x<3) → L1 : COND` | `label = Label1; continue` vs `break` |\n\nThe two places C leaves the body (`continue` at `label2`, falling off the end) are exactly the two `break`s; the backward `goto label1` is an inner-loop `continue`, so it correctly does **not** re-test the `while` condition. Guards, order, and the three literals match. `x--` runs only when `x > 0` (never overflows); `y--` can hit `INT_MIN` only when `y < 0` on entry (C UB), where Rust's `wrapping_sub` matches what gcc emits.\n\n**Empirical verification** (scratch harness in `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-1bfdsY/tmp/audit/`, nothing under `c_src/`, `src/`, `tests/` touched — mtimes unchanged):\n\n- **4409 pairs** compared byte-for-byte via `dlopen` of both `.so`s: exhaustive `x,y ∈ [-8,60]` minus `x>0&&y<0`, an 11×13 extremes grid (`INT_MIN`, `INT_MIN+1`, `-1000000`, `0..5`, `1000`, `100000`), and 10 multi-MiB cases (up to ~7 MB, crossing many stdio flushes). **0 divergences.**\n- **10 capped-prefix cases** at 1 MiB each for the ~2^31-iteration region (never run to completion): `(1,-1)`, `(5,-3)`, `(1,INT_MIN)`, `(4,INT_MIN+1)`, `(2,INT_MIN)`, `(INT_MAX,0)`, `(INT_MAX,5)`, `(INT_MAX,INT_MAX)`, `(0,INT_MAX)`, `(INT_MIN,INT_MAX)`. **All byte-identical.**\n- Repeated against C rebuilt at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os` and against **both** `target/debug` and `target/release` Rust. All 0 divergences.\n- **Artifact integrity:** the C `.so`'s `driver` disassembles to a literal instruction-for-instruction match of `driver.c` (at `-O0`), and the shipped `target/debug/libdriver.so` matches a fresh `rustc` build of `src/lib.rs` on the whole grid — so neither side is stale.\n- Every expected-output string cited in ERRORS.md rows 5–12 verified against the real C library — all correct.\n\n**The one divergence found.** `gcc` rewrites `printf(\"loop\\n\")` at `driver.c:31` into `puts(\"loop\")` (visible in the disassembly; happens at every `-O`). glibc's `puts` emits the body and the appended `'\\n'` as **two** `write(2)` calls when stdout is unbuffered; the Rust `printf(\"%s\", \"loop\\n\")` emits **one**. With `setvbuf(stdout,NULL,_IONBF,0)` in a fresh process and fd 1 a `SOCK_SEQPACKET` socket (one datagram per write), `driver(2,2)`:\n\n```\nC          : 10 writes  4:\"loop\" 1:\"\\n\" 1:\"x\" 1:\"\\n\" 1:\"y\" 1:\"\\n\" 1:\"x\" 1:\"\\n\" 1:\"y\" 1:\"\\n\"\nRust debug :  5 writes  5:\"loop\\n\" 2:\"x\\n\" 2:\"y\\n\" 2:\"x\\n\" 2:\"y\\n\"\n```\n\nThis becomes a genuine **byte-stream** difference once fd 1 is shared with another writer. Unbuffered pipe, `driver(600,0)`, plus a concurrent noise writer:\n\n```\nC          : 6600 bytes, 600 \"loop\" tokens, 457 torn (C's \"loop\" and \"\\n\" split by noise)\nRust debug : 6600 bytes, 600 \"loop\" tokens,   0 torn\nRust release: same as C (404 vs 409 torn)\n```\n\nScope: `target/release/libdriver.so` imports `puts` (LLVM applies the same `printf`→`puts` simplification at opt-level ≥ 1) and is syscall-identical to C; only `target/debug/libdriver.so` imports `printf` and differs — and that debug artifact is precisely the one the project's own suite tests. Byte streams are identical for every single-writer configuration I tried (fully buffered file, `_IOLBF`, `_IONBF`, real pty, pipe, `_exit()` without flush, `dlopen`ed repeatedly). Severity: low/exotic; fidelity fix would be to call `puts` for these three literals, or to note that release is the intended artifact.\n\n## (b) Gaps in ERRORS.md / CONFIGS.md\n\n1. **Huge positive `y` as a ~2^31-iteration shape is in neither document.** ERRORS.md row 15 names only `x == INT_MAX` / \"any huge `x > 0`\", but `x <= 0 && y == INT_MAX` drains `y` one per back-edge pass — equally ~2^31 iterations, ~4 GB. `INT_MAX` never appears anywhere in CONFIGS.md (row 19's \"F extremes\" lists only `INT_MIN` pairs; row 20 tops out at `y = 400_000`). It *is* tested (`tests/error_paths.rs:245-246`), so this is a documentation gap, not a test gap.\n2. **`x == 1 && y == 4` reached *below* the check, via the back-edge, is never named.** The special-case test sits above `label1`, so a state of `x==1, y==4` arriving at `label1`/`label2` must **not** re-trigger `goto label2`. It is reachable: entry `(2,5)` re-enters `label1` with exactly `x==1, y==4`. CONFIGS axis B lists only entry-time states (\"true / false via `x!=1` / false via `y!=4`\"), and ERRORS rows 9-10 likewise. Neither doc states the (correct) reachability argument that `(1,4)` can never recur at the *top* of the `while` body. CONFIGS.md's mutation list also has no mutation that hoists/duplicates the special-case test into the label1 dispatch — the exact mistranslation this case guards against (`(2,5)` in row 11 would catch it, incidentally).\n3. **stdout buffering mode and the *kind* of fd 1 are not an axis at all.** CONFIGS axis H is only output *volume*; §\"How the comparison works\" pins fd 1 to a temp file or a pipe (both fully buffered). Untested/undocumented: `_IONBF`, `_IOLBF`, fd 1 = TTY, fd 1 = datagram/seqpacket socket, fd 1 closed, fd 1 = pipe with no reader (EPIPE/SIGPIPE), a caller sharing fd 1 with another writer, and `_exit()` without `fflush`. This is exactly where the divergence above lives. Relatedly, **SYMBOLS.md's claim is too strong**: `puts(\"loop\")` and `printf(\"%s\",\"loop\\n\")` are byte-identical only at the buffered-stream level, not at `write(2)` framing, and not once a second writer shares fd 1. (I checked all of these; only the `_IONBF` framing case differs.)\n4. **Concurrency is not covered.** CONFIGS axis G is only sequential/interleaved *single-threaded* calls. Concurrent calls from multiple threads sharing the stdio lock appear in neither document.\n5. **The C build configuration is treated as fixed but isn't.** CONFIGS.md concludes \"one C build configuration\" from the absence of `option()`/`if()` in `CMakeLists.txt`, but `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` is an implicit knob of every CMake project (`c_src/build/CMakeCache.txt` carries `-O3 -DNDEBUG` etc., and the reference `.so` was built with an *empty* build type, i.e. no `-O` — which neither doc records). This matters because ERRORS.md rows 14/15 justify `wrapping_sub` by appealing to \"the two's-complement wraparound **gcc emits here**\" — a claim about one compiler at one optimization level. I rebuilt at `-O0/-O1/-O2/-O3/-Os` and found no behavioral difference on the bounded grid or the UB prefixes, so this is an analysis gap, not a live bug.\n6. **The write-error surface is absent from ERRORS.md**, which for a `void`, stdout-only API is arguably its only real error path: EPIPE/SIGPIPE, closed fd 1, ENOSPC. I tested EPIPE and closed fd 1 — identical (both die with SIGPIPE 13; both exit 0 with fd 1 closed) — but no row covers it.\n\nEverything else in both documents checks out: all 5 C guards are enumerated, all cited expected outputs are correct, and rows 14/15's narrative (\"prints `loop`, `x`, then `y` forever\" for `x>0 && y<0`) matches the real library (`(1,-1)` → `loop\\nx\\ny\\ny\\ny\\n…`).\n\n## (c) External-caller concerns in the Rust\n\n- **Export is correct.** `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern \"C\" fn driver(c_int, c_int)` → `T driver` in `nm -D`, signature matches `driver.h`. Verified by calling through `dlsym` only, never directly.\n- **No reachable panic path.** The only assertion is `debug_assert_eq!(s.last(), Some(&0u8))` in `c_print`, applied to three static NUL-terminated literals — statically unreachable. It is also dead weight worth deleting.\n- **No unwinding across FFI.** Even if a panic occurred, `extern \"C\"` carries Rust's abort-on-unwind shim (debug uses `panic=unwind`; release sets `panic = \"abort\"`), so it aborts rather than unwinding into C. Not UB.\n- **No buffering surprise, no SIGPIPE surprise.** Rust std does *not* install `SIG_IGN` for SIGPIPE in a `cdylib` (no `lang_start`), so EPIPE kills the caller identically to the C library — verified.\n- **No hidden state.** 4409 calls per library, alternating C/Rust in a single process, produced identical output every time.\n- **Latent format-string asymmetry.** C passes the payload *as the format string* (`printf(\"loop\\n\")`); Rust passes it as a `%s` argument. Identical for these three literals (no `%`), but the two would diverge the moment the payload became dynamic — worth a comment, since the current comment claims the `%s` form is for fidelity when it is actually a deliberate deviation.\n- **`printf` vs `puts` framing** (see (a)) — the only measured behavioral difference; debug-profile only.\n- **Footprint / runtime deps:** 3.9 MB debug, 393 KB release vs 15 KB for C; the Rust `.so` imports `pthread_*`, `_Unwind_*`, `malloc`, `writev` and needs `libgcc_s`, where the C `.so` imports only `puts`. This can matter in minimal-runtime or sandboxed consumers, and `dlclose`ing a Rust `cdylib` is riskier than the C one (Rust std registers `atexit`/TLS destructors) — no document covers `dlclose` or repeated load/unload.\n- **The hangs are faithfully reproduced.** `x>0 && y<0`, huge `x`, and huge `y` run ~2^31 iterations in *both* libraries. Correct for fidelity, but a drop-in consumer inherits the hang.","usage":{"total_tokens":126455,"tool_uses":46,"duration_ms":1037859},"uuid":"cefc9e23-8571-480c-aa83-92789883de4d","session_id":"eeb44132-fb7e-4437-9dd9-10fc85cd1842"}
